@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+# Owned process lifecycle acceptance
+
+The v3 review found that a normal runner exit could certify only the PIDs already listed. Electron may start a utility/renderer later without using Node child_process. If close or app.evaluate throws, those late processes may outlive the main PID. A wrapper SIGTERM also previously bypassed asynchronous cleanup.
+
+Required shared boundary (implementation under review):
+
+1. Before dispatch, require a readable process inventory and a matching UID/birth identity for the caller. Inspection denial starts no workload. Record each actual spawn with PID and birth stamp. Record the detached dispatch process group and the exact isolated user-data profile observed in owned launch arguments.
+2. Capture descendants at Node exit / Electron will-quit, and before timeout or catchable outer interruption. No periodic process scanning runs inside measurement windows.
+3. After runner exit, scan process metadata in memory. Match only this UID, the owned group or exact profile, and a birth stamp no earlier than its owner. Reject reused leader identities; never kill based only on a PID, process name, or profile prefix.
+4. TERM then KILL only an identity still matching its recorded birth stamp. Confirm exit of every registered identity. Repeat bounded residual scans until two successive scans are empty. Scan denial, malformed/truncated rows, missing ownership identity, continued spawning, or failed termination means unconfirmed; keep the shared native lock blocked and stop the queue.
+5. Signal handlers are installed before inspection, spawn and synchronous ownership/output setup; the whole startup is inside the same try/finally. Outer SIGINT/SIGTERM follows the same capture/termination/confirmation path and throws `NativeExecutionInterrupted` with `error.result.cleanup` after cleanup. Wrappers consume that proof before their `finally` releases or retains the lease, then rethrow; the shared queue refuses another dispatch. Post-spawn setup errors also terminate the identity-bound child and preserve any incomplete ownership evidence as blocked. An uncatchable SIGKILL cannot run cleanup and must leave the existing lock as unresolved.
+6. If identity becomes unavailable after dispatch, return within the bounded termination window, destroy the caller's pipe handles, and unref the child handle. This is only a bounded-return mechanism: it does not claim that the workload exited, change `cleanup.confirmed: false`, or release the blocked lock.
+
+Process listings are discarded after filtering; no unrelated command line is written to public output. Private ownership receipts contain only owned PID/birth/profile metadata. Native verification remains required: Node simulators exercise lifecycle mechanics, not every future Chromium launch implementation.
+
+Regression fixtures: a late orphan with the owned profile after main exit; an unprofiled child remaining in the owned detached process group; a reused leader PID; another UID; a profile-prefix collision; unknown birth stamp; scan failure; TERM ignored; SIGTERM of the outer wrapper after ready and during synchronous startup; post-spawn receipt-write failure. Each real-process fixture cleans up only its own nonce/PID after its assertion. A full-walk with unconfirmed process cleanup is `broken`, even when its functional report was clean.
